      "side": 1,
      "message": "I gave this a spin and compared this with luci-test-results\n\n```\n# Comparison of Skills Results for PS5\n\nThis document compares the results obtained using the `buildbucket` skill and the `luci-test-results` skill for analyzing the failures on patchset 5 of CL 7474058.\n\n## 1. Overview of Findings\n\n| Feature | Buildbucket Skill (`bb_wrapper.py`) | LUCI Test Results Skill (`luci_triage.py`) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Failed Builds Found** | 4 unique failing builders listed. | 5 failing builds listed (revealed a retry for `android-x86-rel`). |\n| **Failed Suites Identified** | `chrome_public_test_apk` and `chrome_public_test_apk_tablet`. | `chrome_public_test_apk` (based on test IDs). |\n| **Failed Test Cases Count** | **17** (in `chrome_public_test_apk` on one build) - *Incomplete*. | **1,014** (in `chrome_public_test_apk` on one build) - *Complete*. |\n\n## 2. Key Differences\n\n*   **Completeness and Accuracy:** The `luci-test-results` skill was much more comprehensive, reporting 1,014 failures. This is because it queries ResultDB directly, which is the absolute source of truth for all test attempts. The `buildbucket` log file reported only 17 failures for the same suite, which was determined to be an **incomplete log** generated by the recipe, likely missing results from crashed or timed-out shards.\n*   **Root Cause Visibility:** The `luci-test-results` output provided immediate visibility into the *nature* of the failures, showing that many were due to an `AfterClassException`. This explained the high count (cascading failures). The `buildbucket` log did not provide this level of detail in the summarized view.\n*   **Ease of Processing:** The `luci-test-results` script produced structured JSON that was smaller and easier to parse (using targeted tools like `grep` or custom scripts). The `buildbucket` JSON log was extremely large (7MB) and yet still incomplete.\n\n## 3. Conclusion\n\n*   **`luci-test-results`** is the superior tool for getting an accurate, complete, and actionable list of test failures. It avoids the risk of looking at incomplete logs generated by the build recipe.\n*   **`buildbucket`** remains useful for a high-level overview of which builders failed across a patchset, but its detailed test logs should be treated with caution when analyzing large-scale failures.\n```\n\nCould we use the descriptions to direct agents to use the better skill for each use case?",
